<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beiteddine Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1788–<a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Beit ed-Dine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Beiteddine Palace is a palace in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chouf">Chouf mountains</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashir-shihab-ii">Bashir Shihab II</a> over some thirty years from about 1788. It combines Italian and Arab architecture around large courtyards, with fountains, mosaics and baths. After the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> it served the Ottoman and later the Lebanese governments and is now a presidential summer residence and museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beiteddine-palace.jpg" alt="Beiteddine Palace" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peripitus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Courtyard_at_Beiteddine_Palace_-_2009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
